  "texts": [
    "Why does a full water bottle burst in the freezer?",
    "Most substances shrink when they freeze.",
    "Water is the odd one: ice needs more space.",
    "Freezing water grows by about nine percent.",
    "It comes down to hydrogen bonds between molecules.",
    "In ice, molecules lock into an open hexagonal lattice.",
    "That lattice holds more empty space than liquid water.",
    "So ice is less dense and floats on water.",
    "A freezer cools down to about minus eighteen degrees.",
    "The bottle freezes from the outside, forming an ice shell.",
    "The water inside expands but has nowhere to go.",
    "Trapped ice can push with up to two thousand atmospheres.",
    "Glass can't stretch, so it cracks.",
    "The same force bursts pipes and splits rocks in winter.",
    "Tip: leave some room at the top of the bottle.",
    "Follow us for more physics from your kitchen!"
  ]
